# Roblox's Client-Server Model & Network Ownership, Explained

Roblox runs a client-server model like most multiplayer engines: the server is authoritative, clients render what the server tells them, done. Except for physics. Physics simulation for any given `BasePart` is delegated to exactly one machine at a time - the server, or a specific client - and which one is holding that responsibility at any moment is called its **network ownership**.

## Why Ownership Exists At All

Simulating physics for every physical object in a game, for every player, entirely on the server, doesn't scale - the server would need to run full rigid-body simulation for parts that only matter to the single player standing near them. Roblox's solution: hand simulation authority for a part to whichever client is physically closest to it, letting that client's machine do the actual physics math and stream the resulting position back to the server.

```lua
-- checking who currently owns a part
local owner = part:GetNetworkOwner()
if owner == nil then
    print("Server owns this part")
else
    print(owner.Name .. " owns this part")
end
```

By default, Roblox auto-assigns ownership based on proximity - the nearest player to an unanchored part typically becomes its owner, and ownership transfers automatically as players move around.

## Where This Gets Exploitable

Here's the part that matters for security: if a client owns a part, that client's machine is the source of truth for that part's physical state, and the server largely trusts what it reports back. A modified client that owns, say, its own player character (which it always does, by default) can report a position anywhere on the map, teleporting instantly from the server's perspective, because the server is trusting client-reported physics data for a part the client legitimately owns.

This is why "anti-teleport" scripts that only check `Humanoid.MoveDirection` or velocity thresholds get bypassed constantly - the exploit isn't moving the character through normal means, it's directly manipulating the CFrame of a part the exploiting client already has ownership authority over.

## Taking Ownership Back Explicitly

For anything security-sensitive - a projectile's damage-dealing hitbox, a currency pickup, a vehicle's collision part - explicitly setting network ownership to the server removes the client's ability to lie about its position:

```lua
-- force server-side simulation, don't trust the client's physics reporting
part:SetNetworkOwner(nil)
```

The tradeoff is real: server-owned parts are simulated on the server, which costs server CPU per part and reintroduces the latency that ownership delegation exists to avoid. The right call is almost always selective - leave cosmetic or non-exploitable parts on client ownership for responsiveness, and force server ownership only on parts where a lying client would actually matter.

## The Takeaway

Network ownership isn't a security feature, it's a performance optimization that happens to have security implications if you don't know it exists. Anything a player can plausibly exploit for advantage - hit detection, currency, movement-adjacent physics - needs either server-side validation that doesn't trust reported positions, or explicit `SetNetworkOwner(nil)` to take the client's authority away entirely.
